#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Batch 3: establishing views, third pass. Fixes: A stranger flora, smaller bladders, a carcass; B alien striders, clearer ringed throats, organic far windpipe; C irregular sacs, no hero sac, no bat silhouette, no portal.
R = '/home/user/workspace/r3/prompts'
HEAD = "A wide 16:9 landscape frame from a big-budget natural-history film about an alien world. It must read as real {night}location photography, not artwork: {cam}, true lens perspective, fine physical surface detail{where}, {haze}, {grain}."
TAIL = "It must feel like a place you could walk into: firm ground in the foreground, a readable route into the middle distance, far landmarks dissolving in the air. Nothing posed, nothing glowing unless it is lit, no painterly brushwork, no illustration styling. No people, no buildings or ruins, no cameras, drones or equipment, no text, no watermark, no border."
A = [HEAD.format(night='', cam='24 mm lens, deep focus, natural light only', where='', haze='humid haze building with distance', grain='slight film grain'),
"""THE PLACE. The inside of the windpipe of a creature the size of a mountain range that died ages ago. It is now a tunnel-cave about two hundred metres high and wide that runs straight into the distance. Its walls and roof are built from regular hoops of pale cartilage, each hoop as thick as a house, with dark wet rock between them, so the whole tunnel is ribbed, ring after ring, like the inside of a hose. Along its length the roof has fallen in at intervals, leaving ragged skylights. Through each skylight the sun comes down in one thick shaft through the wet air, and under each shaft an island of rainforest has grown on the rubble. It is not an Earth forest: tree ferns and broad-leaved giants stand among pale-trunked trees with dark wine-red new leaves, and long ribbon vines hang from the skylight edges. Thin waterfalls drop through the skylights. A river runs along the tunnel floor between the islands. The tunnel breathes: a slow tongue of cloud slides along under the roof toward the viewer and curls around the skylights.""",
"""VIEWPOINT AND LAYOUT, asymmetric, nothing centred. Seen from standing height on mossy rubble at the river's edge, in the dark stretch of tunnel before the first skylight. The river runs from the bottom left corner away down the tunnel: the obvious route into the picture. Low on the right, in shade, a fallen hoop segment covered in moss carries a colony of pale bladders like puffballs, from the size of a fist to the size of a person, crowded together; a few have split and breathe out fine golden dust that drifts toward the light. On the near bank beside them lies the carcass of a large six-legged animal, half sunk in moss, the same pale bladders growing out of its ribs. In the middle distance the first forest island stands in its shaft of sun, right of centre, its trees tiny under the roof, a flock of small four-winged animals circling up the shaft. Far beyond it, much smaller and paler in the haze, a second shaft of light falls on a second island, and beyond that a third is barely visible. The ribbed walls curve up on both sides and are lost in darkness and cloud overhead.""",
"""LIGHT AND WEATHER. The only light is daylight from the skylights: hard warm sun in the shafts, soft green bounce light near them, deep cool darkness between, wet highlights on rock and leaves, mist in every beam. Colours: deep green, wet black rock, pale bone, dark wine red in small amounts, a little gold. The mood is vast, beautiful and lethal."""]
B = [HEAD.format(night='', cam='24 mm lens, deep focus, natural light only', where='', haze='sea haze building with distance', grain='slight film grain'),
"""THE PLACE. A tidal shelf on a cold coast at low tide, soon after sunrise, sea fog burning off. The shelf is the exposed lung of a creature the size of a mountain range that died ages ago: bone-white, porous and honeycombed like weathered coral limestone, tide-stained and streaked with droppings. It is completely overgrown with animal life, not plants: wide carpets of crimson feathery gill-plumes like the crowns of giant tube worms, beds of pale barnacle-like cones, black-green ribbon weed, orange crusts. Set into the shelf are the open ends of the airways: enormous round throats twenty to sixty metres across. Each is a low broken collar of ivory cartilage, plainly built from stacked rings like the cut end of a giant ribbed hose, none taller than a house, all tilted at slightly different angles, with a dark hole in the middle. Every throat is exhaling at the same moment: a dense column of cold white vapour pours up out of each dark hole and leans the same way on the wind.""",
"""VIEWPOINT AND LAYOUT, asymmetric, nothing centred. Seen from standing height at the edge of a tide channel. The channel of pale teal seawater runs from the bottom centre away across the shelf, bending between the throats toward the far distance: the obvious route into the picture. On the right, about forty metres away, the nearest throat is seen clearly: its ringed outer wall, its wet rim, the dark opening, crimson plumes crowding the rim and bending outward in the rush of air. On the left bank in the foreground the crimson plume carpet comes right up to the viewer's feet, wet and glistening. More than twenty other exhaling throats stand at every distance along the channel, shrinking and paling into the fog. In the middle distance five tall pale striders wade the channel in a loose line: animals like giant sea spiders, each a body no bigger than a barrel carried eight metres up on eight very long, thin, jointed legs, with no neck and no head to speak of. A flock of hundreds of small dark flyers wheels through one distant column. At the far end of the channel, faint in the fog, the coast rises into hills, and out of the hillside lies the broken end of the main windpipe: one ribbed tube of pale cartilage hundreds of metres across, on its side like a fallen tower, eroded, cracked and overgrown, its dark mouth breathing out the fog bank itself.""",
"""LIGHT AND WEATHER. A low sun, back left, just breaking through the fog: warm pale-gold light edges every vapour column and lays long reflections on the wet shelf; shadows stay cold blue-grey. Colours: arterial crimson in broad masses, ivory bone, pale teal water, wet black, small patches of orange. The mood is cold, quiet and lethal."""]
C = [HEAD.format(night='night-time ', cam='fast 24 mm lens at high sensitivity, available light only', where=' where light reaches it', haze='mist thickening with distance', grain='visible sensor noise in the shadows'),
"""THE PLACE. Deep dusk in a shallow valley on the body of a creature the size of a mountain range that died ages ago. Its lung has weathered open here, and the valley floor is crowded with its air sacs, still alive: thousands of soft membrane bladders pressed together in heaps like frogspawn or clustered puffballs, joined by ropes of wet tissue, from the size of a barrel to the size of a house. None is a neat dome: they sag, wrinkle, bulge and lean on each other. Their skins are thin, leathery and wet, with a few branching veins, pale where stretched and dark where slack. They breathe: at this moment some are swollen tight and others have collapsed into folds. Colonies of luminous organisms live inside them, so each sac glows faintly from within, cold blue-green with a dim amber core, unevenly. Inside several, against the light, hang the small dark shapes of caught animals with too many thin limbs, half dissolved. The sacs exhale a low mist through puckered pores; it lies knee-deep over the ground, catches their light and drains slowly downhill.""",
"""VIEWPOINT AND LAYOUT, asymmetric, nothing centred. Seen from standing height on a wet black rock path. The path runs from the bottom right away down the valley between the heaps of sacs, beside a narrow black stream that mirrors the glow: the obvious route into the picture. No single sac dominates: the nearest heap is on the left, four or five sacs the size of small cars within arm's reach, near enough to see their veins, the beads of moisture on them and one jet of mist leaving a pore. The heaps recede down the valley at every distance, dimmer and bluer in the mist, hundreds of soft lights, climbing the far slope. Small pale moth-like animals spiral toward several of them. The valley is closed at the far end by a dark cliff pocked with ragged cave mouths that breathe out mist. The sky is the last band of deep blue and dull orange twilight low on the horizon, with the first stars above.""",
"""LIGHT AND EXPOSURE. Exposed for the living light, so most of the frame is dark: blue-black land, soft cyan-green pools of light on mist and wet rock around each heap, a few amber points. The glow is weak, falls off quickly and is never even. The mood is silent, beautiful and lethal."""]
for k, parts in {'A-est-v3': A, 'B-est-v3': B, 'C-est-v3': C}.items():
    open(f'{R}/{k}.txt', 'w').write('\n\n'.join(parts) + '\n\n' + TAIL + '\n'); print(k, sum(map(len, parts)))
